roke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="68" cy="248" r="1.6" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="80" y="248" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">B = 0.10 T</text></svg>', 'A singly charged ion of mass 4.0 × 10⁻²⁶ kg enters a uniform 0.10 T field (out of the page) through a slit at 1.0 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹ and travels a semicircle to the detector.'),
('55decc7d-7f29-522c-9b5a-b4dfd605d465', '3fd36cc2-d7e7-4424-b4b5-11a4a8b7ac04', 'ca14c791-7996-5cb6-9fb9-b8764146fc83', 'a-level', '25.1', '9702 coursebook 25.1 · Force on a moving charge', 49, 49, 'Electrons are accelerated from rest through 1.2 kV and then enter a field of 0.80 mT at right angles. (a) Calculate their speed. (b) Calculate the magnetic force on each electron, in units of 10⁻¹⁵ N. (c) Calculate the acceleration. (d) Explain why they move in a circle. Give only the answer to (b). Take e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C and mₑ = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '2.628', '10⁻¹⁵ N', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) v = √(2 × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 1200 ÷ 9.11 × 10⁻³¹) = 2.05 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹. (b) F = 0.80 × 10⁻³ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 2.053 × 10⁷ = 2.63 × 10⁻¹⁵ N. (c) a = F/m = 2.628 × 10⁻¹⁵ ÷ 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ = 2.88 × 10¹⁵ m s⁻². (d) The force is constant in size and always perpendicular to the velocity, so it is a centripetal force. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: ½mv² = eV. C1: F = BQv. C1: a = F/m. B1: perpendicular constant force. B1: centripetal. A1: F = 2.63 × 10⁻¹⁵ N.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('c67ef2ac-fcd5-52da-868a-e45b3070758e', '3fd36cc2-d7e7-4424-b4b5-11a4a8b7ac04', 'ca14c791-7996-5cb6-9fb9-b8764146fc83', 'a-level', '25.1', '9702 coursebook 25.1 · Force on a moving charge', 50, 50, 'A proton and an α-particle (charge 2e, mass 4mₚ) are accelerated from rest through the same p.d. and enter the same magnetic field at right angles. (a) Show that the α-particle''s speed is 1/√2 of the proton''s. (b) Calculate (magnetic force on α-particle) ÷ (magnetic force on proton). Give only the answer to (b).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.414', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) ½mv² = QV gives v = √(2QV/m); for the α-particle Q/m is half that of the proton, so v is 1/√2 as large. (b) F = BQv: ratio = 2 × (1/√2) = √2 = 1.41. Mark scheme (5 marks). M1: v = √(2QV/m). B1: v ratio 1/√2. C1: F ∝ Qv. C1: ratio. A1: ratio = 1.41.', 5, NULL, NULL)
