('8690bb73-5f7d-50a2-a547-73cd74a05720', '3fd36cc2-d7e7-4424-b4b5-11a4a8b7ac04', '9a577035-5226-4d06-b1f8-39a2b1f93b0a', 'a-level', '25.2', '9702 coursebook 25.2 · Orbiting charged particles', 28, 28, 'An electron and a proton circle in the same magnetic field. Calculate (period of electron) ÷ (period of proton). Take mₑ = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg and mₚ = 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ kg.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '5.455e-4', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'T = 2πm/BQ ∝ m for equal |Q|: ratio = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ ÷ 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ = 5.46 × 10⁻⁴. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: T ∝ m. C1: ratio. A1: ratio = 5.46 × 10⁻⁴.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('434ecbd0-336f-50ec-b99d-c9550569d8f5', '3fd36cc2-d7e7-4424-b4b5-11a4a8b7ac04', '9a577035-5226-4d06-b1f8-39a2b1f93b0a', 'a-level', '25.2', '9702 coursebook 25.2 · Orbiting charged particles', 29, 29, 'Why does the time for one orbit not change when a charged particle in a uniform magnetic field speeds up?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Its radius increases in proportion to its speed, so the circumference grows at the same rate as the speed', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'T = 2πr/v = 2πm/BQ, independent of v. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: r ∝ v. C1: T = 2πr/v constant. A1: A (Its radius increases in proportion to its speed, so the circumference grows at the same rate as the speed).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('95d06a0c-b9ea-597e-9a15-dac114107485', '3fd36cc2-d7e7-4424-b4b5-11a4a8b7ac04', '9a577035-5226-4d06-b1f8-39a2b1f93b0a', 'a-level', '25.2', '9702 coursebook 25.2 · Orbiting charged particles', 30, 30, 'A proton and an α-particle (mass 6.64 × 10⁻²⁷ kg, charge 2e) move at the same speed at right angles to the same field. Calculate (radius for α-particle) ÷ (radius for proton). Take mₚ = 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ kg.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.988', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'r ∝ m/Q: ratio = (6.64 × 10⁻²⁷ ÷ 2) ÷ 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ = 1.99. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: r ∝ m/Q. C1: substitution. A1: ratio = 1.99.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('fef212d0-5ed3-59b0-af0b-ea483c1b2c62', '3fd36cc2-d7e7-4424-b4b5-11a4a8b7ac04', '9a577035-5226-4d06-b1f8-39a2b1f93b0a', 'a-level', '25.2', '9702 coursebook 25.2 · Orbiting charged particles', 31, 31, 'A cyclotron has dees of radius 0.50 m in a field of 1.5 T. Calculate the maximum kinetic energy of the protons it produces, in MeV (1 MeV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹³ J). Take e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C and mₚ = 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ kg.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '26.95', 'MeV', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'v_max = BQr/m = 1.5 × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 0.50 ÷ 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ = 7.19 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹. E_k = 0.5 × 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ × (7.186 × 10⁷)² = 4.31 × 10⁻¹² J. In MeV: 4.311 × 10⁻¹² ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻¹³ = 26.9. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: v = BQr/m. C1: ½mv². C1: conversion to MeV. A1: E_k = 26.9 MeV.', 4, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 310" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg1df43e6b-t fg1df43e6b-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg1df43e6b-t">Cyclotron</title><desc id="fg1df43e6b-d">Top view of a cyclotron: two hollow D-shaped electrodes (dees) of radius 0.50 m with a gap between them, in a uniform 1.5 T field out of the page. An alternating p.d. across the gap accelerates protons along a spiral path.</desc><path d="M172,25 A105,105 0 0,0 172,235 Z" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><path d="M188,25 A105,105 0 0,1 188,235 Z" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polyline points="180,118 180.2,117.9 180.4,117.8 180.6,117.7 180.9,117.6 181.1,117.5 181.3,117.4 181.6,117.3 181.8,117.2 182,117.2 182.3,117.1 182.5,117 182.8,117 183,116.9 183.3,116.8 183.5,116.8 183.8,116.8 184.1,116.7 184.3,116.7 184.6,116.7 184.9,116.6 185.1,116.6 185.4,116.6 185.7,116.6 186,116.6 186.2,116.6 186.5,116.6 186.8,116.6 187.