('0e1045a3-0b9d-5649-a47e-0345bf531b0d', '3fd36cc2-d7e7-4424-b4b5-11a4a8b7ac04', '9a577035-5226-4d06-b1f8-39a2b1f93b0a', 'a-level', '25.2', '9702 coursebook 25.2 · Orbiting charged particles', 36, 36, 'A charged particle moves in a circle of radius 0.020 m and completes each orbit in 1.0 × 10⁻⁷ s. Calculate its speed.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.257e6', 'm s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'v = 2πr/T = 2π × 0.020 ÷ 1.0 × 10⁻⁷ = 1.26 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: v = 2πr/T. C1: substitution. A1: v = 1.26 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('a4be1e0d-cca6-55be-94be-d336e1133ec3', '3fd36cc2-d7e7-4424-b4b5-11a4a8b7ac04', '9a577035-5226-4d06-b1f8-39a2b1f93b0a', 'a-level', '25.2', '9702 coursebook 25.2 · Orbiting charged particles', 37, 37, 'An electron completes one orbit in a uniform magnetic field every 3.57 × 10⁻⁹ s. Calculate the flux density, in mT. Take e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C and mₑ = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '10.02 mT', 'mT', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'B = 2πm/(QT) = 2π × 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ ÷ (1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 3.57 × 10⁻⁹) = 0.0100 T. In mT: 0.01002 × 1000 = 10.0. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: rearranged T = 2πm/BQ. C1: conversion. A1: B = 10.0 mT.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('a2495af5-4fbf-518f-9052-adadacaf16f1', '3fd36cc2-d7e7-4424-b4b5-11a4a8b7ac04', '9a577035-5226-4d06-b1f8-39a2b1f93b0a', 'a-level', '25.2', '9702 coursebook 25.2 · Orbiting charged particles', 38, 38, 'The direction of the magnetic field acting on a circling charged particle is reversed. What happens to its orbit?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'It has the same radius but is traversed in the opposite sense', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'The size of BQv is unchanged; its direction reverses. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: same |F|. C1: opposite sense. A1: C (It has the same radius but is traversed in the opposite sense).', 3, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 320 260" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg08b958a1-t fg08b958a1-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg08b958a1-t">Circling particle</title><desc id="fg08b958a1-d">A charged particle moving clockwise in a circle in a uniform field directed into the page (before the field is reversed).</desc><line x1="30" y1="27.5" x2="38" y2="35.5" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="30" y1="35.5" x2="38" y2="27.5" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="30" y1="62.5" x2="38" y2="70.5" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="30" y1="70.5" x2="38" y2="62.5" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="30" y1="97.5" x2="38" y2="105.5" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="30" y1="105.5" x2="38" y2="97.5" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-l