('d1387d23-0d6a-5874-99ac-fcc2beb834d4', '3fd36cc2-d7e7-4424-b4b5-11a4a8b7ac04', '9a577035-5226-4d06-b1f8-39a2b1f93b0a', 'a-level', '25.2', '9702 coursebook 25.2 · Orbiting charged particles', 39, 39, 'A deuteron (mass 2mₚ, charge e) and a proton are accelerated from rest through the same p.d. and enter the same magnetic field. Calculate (radius for deuteron) ÷ (radius for proton).', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.414', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'r = mv/BQ with v = √(2QV/m) gives r = √(2mV/Q)/B, so r ∝ √(m/Q): ratio = √2 = 1.41. Mark scheme (4 marks). M1: r = √(2mV/Q)/B. C1: r ∝ √m for equal Q. C1: ratio. A1: ratio = 1.41.', 4, NULL, NULL),
('1eda3c5e-3bd6-5f08-a295-b425fa39ff54', '3fd36cc2-d7e7-4424-b4b5-11a4a8b7ac04', '9a577035-5226-4d06-b1f8-39a2b1f93b0a', 'a-level', '25.2', '9702 coursebook 25.2 · Orbiting charged particles', 40, 40, 'An electron moves at 1.0 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹ at right angles to the Earth''s field of 5.0 × 10⁻⁵ T. Calculate the radius of its path. Take mₑ = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg and e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.1139', 'm', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'r = mv/BQ = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ × 1.0 × 10⁶ ÷ (1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 5.0 × 10⁻⁵) = 0.114 m. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: r = mv/BQ. C1: substitution. A1: r = 0.114 m.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('ee3c980b-86e4-573a-8575-9b230750242c', '3fd36cc2-d7e7-4424-b4b5-11a4a8b7ac04', '9a577035-5226-4d06-b1f8-39a2b1f93b0a', 'a-level', '25.2', '9702 coursebook 25.2 · Orbiting charged particles', 41, 41, 'Several different particles circle in the same uniform magnetic field. Which quantity is the same for all those with the same charge-to-mass ratio?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The time for one orbit', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'T = 2πm/BQ depends only on m/Q and B. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: T depends on m/Q. C1: not on speed. A1: A (The time for one orbit).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('6bec29d6-d3a7-5f98-8d20-ad563c012b67', '3fd36cc2-d7e7-4424-b4b5-11a4a8b7ac04', '9a577035-5226-4d06-b1f8-39a2b1f93b0a', 'a-level', '25.2', '9702 coursebook 25.2 · Orbiting charged particles', 42, 42, 'Find the kinetic energy, in eV, of an electron whose path in a 1.0 mT field is a circle of radius 5.0 cm. Take e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C and mₑ = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '219.5', 'eV', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'v = BQr/m = 1.0 × 10⁻³ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 0.050 ÷ 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ = 8.78 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹. E_k = 0.5 × 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ × (8.782 × 10⁶)² = 3.51 × 10⁻¹⁷ J. In eV: 3.513 × 10⁻¹⁷ ÷ 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 220. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: v = BQr/m. C1: E_k in eV. A1: E_k = 220 eV.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('4ca9384b-374a-5ec5-b04a-2adf185d93c8', '3fd36cc2-d7e7-4424-b4b5-11a4a8b7ac04', '9a577035-5226-4d06-b1f8-39a2b1f93b0a', 'a-level', '25.2', '9702 coursebook 25.2 · Orbiting charged particles', 43, 43, 'Singly charged magnesium ions of masses 3.98 × 10⁻²⁶ kg (²⁴Mg⁺) and 4.32 × 10⁻²⁶ kg (²⁶Mg⁺) are accelerated from rest through 1000 V and enter a field of 0.25 T at right angles. They travel through a semicircle to a detector. (a) Show that r = √(2mV/Q)/B. (b) Calculate the radius for each isotope. (c) Calculate the separation of the two spots on the detector, in mm. Give only the answer to (c). Take e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '7.466 mm', 'mm', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) v = √(2QV/m) and r = mv/BQ give r = √(2mV/Q)/B. (b) r₂₄ = √(2 × 3.98 × 10⁻²⁶ × 1000 ÷ 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) ÷ 0.25 = 0.0892 m; r₂₆ = √(2 × 4.32 × 10⁻²⁶ × 1000 ÷ 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) ÷ 0.25 = 0.0930 m. (c) Separation = 2 × (0.09295 − 0.08922) = 0.00747 m. In mm: 0.007466 × 1000 = 7.47. Mark scheme (6 marks). B1: r expression derived. C1: r₂₄. C1: r₂₆. M1: separation = 2Δr. C1: conversion. A1: Δx = 7.47 mm.', 6, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 420 260" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg55d8be4c-t fg55d8be4c-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg55d8be4c-t">Magnesium isotopes</title><desc id="fg55d8be4c-d">Singly charged ²⁴Mg⁺ and ²⁶Mg⁺ ions accelerated through 1000 V enter a uniform 0.25 T field and travel semicircles to a detector; the heavier ions travel the larger semicircle.</desc><rect x="60" y="20" width="340" height="180" rx="0" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:5 4"/><circle cx="77" cy="38" r="4.5" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="77" cy="38" r