('e7c8682c-445a-556f-a6bf-5b99f62e125f', '3fd36cc2-d7e7-4424-b4b5-11a4a8b7ac04', '939a9c6c-194e-484e-83e2-0ab494ffa0d4', 'a-level', '25.3', '9702 coursebook 25.3 · Electric and magnetic fields', 8, 8, 'In a velocity selector, what condition must be met for a charged particle to pass through undeflected?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The electric force EQ equals the magnetic force BQv and acts in the opposite direction', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'EQ = BQv gives v = E/B. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: C (The electric force EQ equals the magnetic force BQv and acts in the opposite direction).', 1, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 440 202" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg229c73e0-t fg229c73e0-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg229c73e0-t">Velocity selector</title><desc id="fg229c73e0-d">A velocity selector: parallel plates (top positive) give an electric field down the page, and a uniform magnetic field points into the page. A particle enters from the left; the dashed line is the undeflected path.</desc><rect x="120" y="42" width="220" height="8" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="120" y="160" width="220" height="8" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="108" y="46" font-size="14" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:700">+</text><text x="108" y="164" font-size="14" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:700">−</text><line x1="139.3" y1="68.3" x2="147.3" y2="76.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="139.3" y1="76.3" x2="147.3" y2="68.3" 